WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.380
Game of Thrones is one of the densest and
most complicated shows on television, and

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.360
each episode is multi-layered and loaded with
meaning.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.540
As the show moves toward its epic conclusion,
here's a look at some of the most important

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.019
lines from the final season.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.700
And in case it's not already obvious: Major
spoilers ahead, so please proceed with caution.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.460
When Emilia Clarke's Daenerys and Kit Harington's
Jon Snow finally arrive at Winterfell, they

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.060
meet the rest of the Starks and try to convince
the Northerners to join their cause.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.040
Early on, Jon mutters something important
to Daenerys:

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.920
"Northerners don't much trust outsiders."

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.160
Daenerys doesn't exactly impress either of
the Stark sisters, especially not Sansa, portrayed

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.380
by actress Sophie Turner.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.960
As you can see by that pronounced side-eye,
she clearly doesn't trust this foreign queen,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.480
despite Jon's ringing endorsement.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.100
This isn't a good sign for Daenerys, but it
might not bode particularly well for Jon,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:48.600
either.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.500
As viewers know, he isn't actually the illegitimate
son of Ned Stark at all.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.100
He's the son of Lyanna Stark and Daenerys'
brother, Rhaegar Targaryen.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.880
This fact gives him the biggest claim to the
Iron Throne.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.020
Once the North discovers Jon's true parentage,
it's entirely possible that they'll abandon

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:03.520
him.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.760
Despite his utter devotion to protecting the
North, he's also utterly devoted to the Queen

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:08.500
of Dragons.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.460
Perhaps they'll see that Jon, who was actually
born as Aegon Targaryen, is ultimately an

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:13.140
outsider.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.920
The reunion between Maisie Williams' Arya
Stark and Jon Snow was a highly anticipated

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:18.420
moment.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.980
After all, Jon gave Arya her very first sword.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.960
Though their time together in the first season
was entirely too brief, it was clear that

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.140
they shared an intense bond.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.020
When they meet again at the Godswood at Winterfell,
it provides one of the show's biggest and

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.900
most satisfying emotional moments.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.280
It even manages to get a rare smile out of
Arya, who has hardened considerably over the

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.360
course of Game of Thrones.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.100
Once the two broach the subject of Sansa and
her clear distrust of Daenerys, Arya defends

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.060
her sister, much to Jon's surprise.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.680
Standing her ground, Arya says of her big
sister:

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.260
"I'm defending our family.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:51.700
So is she."

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.479
Of course, she has absolutely no idea that
the man standing in front of her isn't part

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:58.700
of her immediate family.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.720
He's technically a Targaryen, not a Stark.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.760
When Jon finally shares the truth of his lineage
with Arya and Sansa, Arya immediately agrees

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.000
to honor his request and keep the big news
a secret.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.360
But acting on her distrust of Daenerys, Sansa
soon shares the revelation with Tyrion, knowing

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.580
full well that doing so will most definitely
set off a chain of events that could ultimately

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.140
prove quite problematic for the Mother of
Dragons.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.099
Fans have been waiting to see what's going
on with Jon and Daenerys ever since their

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.160
first tryst at the end of the seventh season.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.150
Well, a scene in the first episode of season
eight gave viewers a sneak peek into their

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.020
rather peculiar courtship rituals, which include
long, romantic dragon-rides through the sky.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.720
After the two lovebirds finally decide to
give their dragons a break, Daenerys spots

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.000
a nearby cave, saying to Jon with a smirk:

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.020
"We could stay a thousand years.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.240
No one would find us."

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.180
Well, there's a reason this romantic scene
might have felt a little familiar to you.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.640
Her comment brings back memories of Rose Leslie's
ill-fated character Ygritte, a Wildling that

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.029
Jon once fell in love with.

00:02:57.029 --> 00:03:00.819
The doomed couple had their first intimate
moment in a cave, and Ygritte also suggested

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:02.140
that they stay there forever.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.580
"I don't ever want to leave this cave, Jon
Snow.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:07.940
Not ever."

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.200
Now that people are starting to learn that
Daenerys is actually Jon's aunt, their romantic

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.640
relationship is probably just as doomed as
the one between Ygritte and Jon.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.549
Maybe Jon should start listening to his lovers,
particularly when they suggest laying low

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:20.180
in caves.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.680
Bran Stark has been a total weirdo ever since
he took up the mantle of the Three-Eyed Raven.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.400
He spent the season eight premiere lurking
all over Winterfell and saying all sorts of

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.280
cryptic things.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.960
One night, he has an encounter with Sam Tarly,
portrayed by John Bradley.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.840
When Sam asks what Bran is up to, he receives
this response:

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.320
"...waiting for an old friend."

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.240
At the end of the episode, Bran's in the courtyard
to witness the arrival of Jaime Lannister.

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:47.180
The last time Jaime saw Bran, he shoved him
out of a high window after Bran caught Jaime

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.700
and Cersei in a rather compromising position.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.860
But the "old friend" Bran is waiting for turns
out to be Theon, who finally makes his way

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:54.820
back to Winterfell.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.530
Driven by a need to redeem himself, Theon
volunteers to lead the team that protects

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.099
Bran from the Night King during the climactic
battle with the White Walkers.

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:06.029
Theon ultimately gives his life to try and
protect his onetime foster brother — completing

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:09.240
his redemption arc, and following another
line with huge importance:

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.340
"You're a good man."

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.640
When Jon Snow returns to the North with Daenerys
Targaryen in tow, he's met with plenty of

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:22.320
disapproval.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.620
One of the loudest voices is that of Lyanna
Mormont, the diminutive but tough Lady of

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.360
House Mormont.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:30.720
When the Northern nobles inside the hall of
Winterfell are confused as to whether they

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.890
should refer to Jon or Daenerys as "Your Grace,"
Lyanna stands up and tells Jon:

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:39.260
"You left the North a king and came back a…

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:41.920
I'm not sure what you are now."

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.920
Unbeknownst to Lyanna, it's a rather pointed
statement, considering that his true parentage

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.350
has been revealed to the audience and other
characters are about to start learning the

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.000
news, too.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.380
Really, he's still "Your Grace," except that
he's not just the rightful King in the North.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.880
He's the King of the Seven Kingdoms.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.540
In an effort to bond with Jon's family, Daenerys
pays a visit to Sansa during the season's

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.550
second episode.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.020
She asks if the two can speak alone.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.780
The Dragon Queen's time at the Stark family
home has been fairly fraught thus far, and

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.060
Sansa has been skeptical of this Southern
newcomer, frustrated by her relationship with

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.040
Jon and highly distrustful of her.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.300
During their first private meeting, Daenerys
seems to charm Sansa quite a bit, telling

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.820
her that she's traveled all this way because
of her love for Jon.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.910
She reveals her softer, more vulnerable side
to Sansa, and Sansa does seem to warm up…

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.000
if only for the moment.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.480
At one point in their conversation, Sansa
says:

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.480
"Men do stupid things for women."

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.960
She's referring to Jon letting Daenerys take
control of the North, but her words could

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.780
practically be a thesis statement on one of
Game of Thrones' underlying themes.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.190
From the attempted murder of Bran to Littlefinger's
many schemes and Jon's dangerous plan to capture

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.840
a lone wight, so many misfortunes have befallen
men as they attempted to impress women.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.280
This one sentence succinctly sums up one of
the show's central concerns.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.400
On the eve of the epic Battle of Winterfell,
most of the characters gather up for one final

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.100
night together.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:03.500
They consider their own mortality, sing, drink,
and try to satisfy their every desire… just

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.000
in case the army of the dead overtakes them
before dawn.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:10.240
During a discussion by the fire, Sam Tarly
chimes in, saying that the loss of memories

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.560
is the worst fate of all, and telling his
comrades:

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:15.340
"That's what death is.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:18.620
Forgetting."

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.540
Sam tells his friends that they all might
they die, leaving their names and legacies

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:23.300
lost to time.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.040
It becomes clear that Sam is determined to
keep the memories of his loved ones alive,

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.400
no matter what happens during the coming battle.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.900
This all fits in quite nicely with one of
the most popular theories about Sam.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.060
Thanks to his education at the Citadel, many
people believe the series concludes with him

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.420
writing the titular "song of ice and fire."

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.390
He might be destined to transcribe all the
heroic acts of the people around him, so their

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:44.720
feats can live on forever.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.840
After his speech, it seems even more likely
that Sam will be the chronicler of the entire

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.060
Game of Thrones story.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.160
Since he's not a strong fighter or contender
for the throne, it seems like historian is

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.520
the perfect role for him to play as this saga
thunders towards its finale.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.220
Here's hoping he's a faster writer than George
R.R. Martin...

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.900
The Battle of Winterfell takes place at the
ancestral home of the Starks, so it's no wonder

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.480
that the family is front and center.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.760
While Sansa is stuck down in the crypts, her
sister Arya is out there fighting for her

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.770
life with a full arsenal of weapons at her
disposal.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:14.970
Since they reunited in season 7, the sisters
have been working together to hold the North

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.380
and protect the Stark family.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:20.180
Now that they've reached adulthood, they seem
to have arrived at a place of mutual respect

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:20.680
and love.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:24.760
This becomes clear when Sansa laments that
she can't be altogether helpful during "The

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:25.380
Long Night."

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.760
Perhaps she even feels a bit jealous of her
bolder, brasher sister.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.600
When Arya gives her sister a dragonglass dagger,
she tells her:

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.940
"Stick 'em with the pointy end."

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:35.200
Sound familiar?

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.880
Back in season 1, Jon gave a small sword to
Arya and offered her the exact same instructions:

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.660
"Stick 'em with the pointy end."

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:46.240
Perhaps Arya is subtly suggesting to Sansa
that she believes they're both equally formidable

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:46.740
warriors.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:50.920
Melisandre came out of the woodwork just before
the Battle at Winterfell began.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.740
She wasted no time showing off, lighting the
weapons of every Dothraki warrior on the frontlines…

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.660
a pretty cool flex, if you ask us.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.940
However, she had plenty more to do during
this battle, and it's all tied to Arya Stark.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.440
After several horrifying and grueling run-ins
with wights, Arya finds a brief moment of

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.100
safety alongside the Hound and Melisandre
in a locked chamber in Winterfell.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.120
For the first time in years, Arya seems just
about ready to throw in the towel.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:18.960
Luckily, Melisandre reminds her of a prophecy
she made in season 3 during their first meeting.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.880
"I see a darkness in you.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.720
And in that darkness, eyes staring back at
me."

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:32.540
"Brown eyes, green eyes, and blue eyes."

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.980
Melisandre pauses before "blue eyes" to suggest
that Arya is the one who will ultimately take

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:40.200
down not only the Night King, but all of his
blue-eyed brethren.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.640
Arya's tryst with Gendry raised plenty of
eyebrows.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:47.320
But it was a serious thrill getting to watch
Arya pursue the object of her desire, and

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:49.400
the night clearly meant a lot to Gendry.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.260
After the Battle of Winterfell, Daenerys names
Gendry the Lord of Storm's End.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:57.300
Gendry immediately finds Arya and tells her
that he loves her, asking for her hand in

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:57.800
marriage.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.900
Any Game of Thrones fan who's been paying
attention to Arya's story knows exactly how

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:03.120
this moment will play out.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:06.560
Even though she clearly cares about Gendry,
she turns him down immediately:

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.180
"I'm not a lady.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.040
I never have been.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:14.560
That's not me."

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.320
This is a subtle callback to a moment she
shared in season one with Ned, after her father

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.260
suggests she might one day become the lady
of a great castle.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.160
“No.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:26.800
That's not me."

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.460
Arya has undergone quite a remarkable transformation
throughout the course of the show, but she

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.340
still knows who she is… and who she isn't.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.020
Sansa Stark's evolution on Game of Thrones
has seen her grow from a whiny pre-teen to

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.580
a consummate survivor and shrewd strategist.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.420
Characters within the show have noticed her
incredible growth just as much as audiences

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:45.920
have.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.020
During the celebrations at Winterfell in the
fourth episode of the eighth season, Sansa

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.830
reunites with the Hound, who references the
abuse she suffered at the hands of her late

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:58.380
husband Ramsay Bolton, a sadistic madman who
brutalized her within an inch of her life.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:02.320
Once she tells the Hound how she dispatched
Ramsay — with hounds — the warrior lets

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.200
out an impressed chuckle and tells her:

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:06.860
"You've changed, little bird."

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.680
He tells Sansa that if she'd left King's Landing
under his protection, she never would have

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.940
had to deal with these horrible men and such
awful circumstances.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.360
But Sansa has a perfect line of reasoning
prepared:

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:22.800
"Without Littlefinger and Ramsay and the rest,
I would have stayed a little bird all my life."

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:27.260
She makes it perfectly clear that this little
bird isn't so little anymore, and can fly

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.480
just as well as anyone — or anything.

